menu "USB HID Descriptor"

    config USB_DESC_MANUFACTURER
        string "Manufacturer"
        default "ESP32"
        help
            USB device manufacturer string.

    config USB_DESC_PRODUCT
        string "Product"
        default "Wireless KVM"
        help
            USB device product string.

    config USB_DESC_SERIAL
        string "Serial Number"
        default "000000000001"
        help
            USB device serial number string.

    config USB_DESC_VID
        hex "Vendor ID"
        default 0x303A
        help
            USB Vendor ID (0x303A = Espressif).

    config USB_DESC_PID
        hex "Product ID"
        default 0x4001
        help
            USB Product ID.

endmenu

menu "USB suspend power management"

    config USB_SUSPEND_LIGHT_SLEEP_ENABLE
        bool "Stop WiFi when the PC USB-suspends"
        default y
        help
            Compiled into both roles (power_manager.c): Device role's
            main.c USB connection, and Host role's optional type-c output
            (usb_device_typec.c, MAX3421E/RP2040-bridge configs only).
            When the connected PC suspends (detected via tud_suspend_cb -
            USB bus-level SUSPEND state, distinct from VBUS/power presence
            - see mds/usb_hid/2026-8-30_Sleep.md), stops WiFi
            (esp_wifi_stop()), restarting it when tud_resume_cb fires.
            Also turns the onboard status LED off/on to match. See
            USB_SUSPEND_ACTUAL_LIGHT_SLEEP below for the separate (default
            off) option to also actually light-sleep the CPU meanwhile.

            This is a build-time kill switch, separate from the runtime
            `usb_suspend_wifi_sleep true/false` mruby DSL directive (Host
            role only, main/mruby_filter.c) - that one lets a script opt
            out per-deployment (e.g. a Host board whose WiFi also carries
            its own real-HID-forwarding duty, where stopping it just
            because the local type-c PC suspended would be harmful);
            turning this Kconfig option off instead removes the feature
            from the firmware entirely, regardless of what any script says.

    config USB_SUSPEND_ACTUAL_LIGHT_SLEEP
        bool "Also esp_light_sleep_start() the CPU while WiFi is stopped"
        depends on USB_SUSPEND_LIGHT_SLEEP_ENABLE
        default n
        help
            When enabled, cycles short timer-woken esp_light_sleep_start()
            slices (esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup() - see power_manager.c)
            while the PC is suspended, instead of just waiting for the
            resume notification with the CPU fully awake.

            KNOWN BROKEN on real ESP32-S3 hardware with a 40MHz crystal
            (this project's default, CONFIG_XTAL_FREQ=40) as of 2026-08-30:
            testing showed esp_light_sleep_start() here corrupts the USB
            peripheral's state - tud_resume_cb fired far too early (~600ms
            into a 2s sleep slice), immediately followed by a spurious
            tud_suspend_cb with a *different* remote_wakeup_en value than
            the real suspend had (not something a real PC suspend/resume
            could produce within tens of milliseconds), and the board never
            recovered. Matches the previously-reported freeze bug
            (espressif/esp-idf#15451, espressif/arduino-esp32#6581) with no
            confirmed-working mitigation wired up here. Also, ESP32-S3 has
            no USB-bus wake source for light sleep in this IDF (no
            esp_sleep_enable_usb_wakeup() exists - checked against
            esp_hw_support/include/esp_sleep.h and soc_caps.h) even when it
            doesn't corrupt anything, so an actual PC resume is only
            noticed once tinyusb's own task gets a scheduling window to
            process it during one of the timer-woken slices - expect up to
            roughly one slice's worth of extra delay (a few seconds), not
            instant resume. Leave this off unless specifically
            experimenting with a fix for the corruption above.

endmenu

menu "mruby filter/route (Host role)"

    config MRUBY_FILTER_ROUTE_ENABLE
        bool "Use mruby script for filter/conv/route instead of filter_rules.h/route_rules.h"
        default y
        help
            Host role (KVM_ROLE=HOST) only. When enabled, hid_forwarder.c
            calls into the embedded mruby script (main/mruby_scripts/default.rb)
            for filter/route decisions instead of the C filter_rules.h/
            route_rules.h path. If the script fails to load/parse at boot,
            it automatically falls back to the C path regardless of this
            setting - this only controls whether mruby is attempted at all.
            Also gates the WebUI (mruby_webui.c, Phase 2): with mruby
            itself compiled out there's nothing for it to edit, so it's a
            no-op in that case too.
            See mds/usb_hid/2026-08-28_mruby_filter_route.md and
            mds/usb_hid/2026-08-30_mruby_phase2_webui.md.

endmenu
